      "message": "Let\u0027s refactor the serialization / deserialization to use `struct`s.\n\nHere\u0027s code from Gemini. Check that it\u0027s correct.\n\n-----\n\n1. **Eradicates Undefined Behavior (UB):** Treating an array of bytes as a typed `struct` via `reinterpret_cast` violates the \"Strict Aliasing Rule.\" `std::memcpy` is explicitly defined by the C++ standard as the safe, legal way to copy object representations.\n2. **Zero-Cost Abstraction:** Modern compilers optimize `std::memcpy` with statically known sizes completely away, translating it directly into register loads and stores. You get perfect safety with zero performance penalty.\n3. **Single Allocation (Zero Heap Churn):** By using `std::span` and our helpers, we allocate a single `std::vector` exactly once per command, neatly packing the header and payload together without triggering dynamic reallocations.\n4. **Self-Documenting \u0026 Safe Initialization:** Magic math (like `offset +\u003d 12`) is replaced by explicit struct layouts. C++20 designated initializers ensure no fields are accidentally left uninitialized with garbage memory.\n5. **Centralized Safety:** The `WriteStruct` and `ReadStruct` helpers enforce bounds checking at compile time (via concepts) and run time (via span sizes), bubbling up errors uniformly via `std::expected`.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: The Core Helpers (`egis_util.h`)\n\nFirst, define the standard-compliant serialization helpers that enforce bounds checking and type safety.\n\n```cpp\n#include \u003cconcepts\u003e\n#include \u003ccstring\u003e\n#include \u003cexpected\u003e\n#include \u003cspan\u003e\n#include \u003csystem_error\u003e\n#include \u003ctype_traits\u003e\n\nnamespace egis {\n\ntemplate \u003ctypename T\u003e\nconcept TriviallyCopyable \u003d std::is_trivially_copyable_v\u003cT\u003e;\n\n// Safely serializes a struct into a byte span.\ntemplate \u003cTriviallyCopyable T\u003e\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, std::errc\u003e WriteStruct(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buffer, const T\u0026 obj, size_t offset \u003d 0) {\n  if (buffer.size() \u003c offset + sizeof(T)) {\n    return std::unexpected(std::errc::no_buffer_space);\n  }\n  std::memcpy(buffer.data() + offset, \u0026obj, sizeof(T));\n  return {};\n}\n\n// Safely deserializes a struct from a byte span.\ntemplate \u003cTriviallyCopyable T\u003e\nstd::expected\u003cT, std::errc\u003e ReadStruct(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buffer, size_t offset \u003d 0) {\n  if (buffer.size() \u003c offset + sizeof(T)) {\n    return std::unexpected(std::errc::message_size);\n  }\n  T obj;\n  std::memcpy(\u0026obj, buffer.data() + offset, sizeof(T));\n  return obj;\n}\n\n}  // namespace egis\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Define the Protocol Structs (`bootrom.h`)\n\nDefine your packets using `__attribute__((packed))` and default member initializers.\n\n```cpp\nnamespace EgisBootrom {\n  struct FlashEraseCommand {\n    uint8_t command_id \u003d kCmdFlashErase;\n    uint32_t address;\n    uint32_t size;\n  } __attribute__((packed));\n}\n\n// Inside class BootromComm:\nstruct SecureCommandShaHeader {\n  uint8_t command_id \u003d EgisBootrom::kCmdSecureWrapped;\n  uint8_t algo;\n  uint32_t payload_len;\n  std::array\u003cuint8_t, kSha256DigestSize\u003e hash{}; // Zero-initializes\n} __attribute__((packed));\n\nstruct SecureShaResponseHeader {\n  uint16_t status;\n  uint32_t payload_len;\n} __attribute__((packed));\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Refactor the Call Sites\n\n#### Implementation A: The EC Response Parser (`ec_comm.cc`)\n\nRemove the UB pointer cast and use our safe `ReadStruct` helper.\n\n```cpp\n// Inside ec_comm.cc : SendCommand()\n\n// ❌ REMOVE THIS:\n// EcHostCommandResponse *resp_hdr \u003d reinterpret_cast\u003cEcHostCommandResponse *\u003e(resp_buf.data());\n\n// ✅ REPLACE WITH:\nauto hdr_res \u003d ReadStruct\u003cEcHostCommandResponse\u003e(resp_buf);\nif (!hdr_res) {\n  return std::unexpected(EcError::kResponseTooShort);\n}\nEcHostCommandResponse resp_hdr \u003d hdr_res.value();\n\nuint16_t result \u003d FromLittleEndian(resp_hdr.result);\nuint16_t data_length \u003d FromLittleEndian(resp_hdr.data_length);\n\n```\n\n#### Implementation B: The SHA256 Secure Command (`bootrom.cc`)\n\nReplace the multiple allocations and offset math with our streamlined stack-to-span pipeline.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e, BootromError\u003e\nBootromComm::SendBootromCommandSha(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data,\n                                   std::chrono::milliseconds timeout) {\n  // --- 1. Serialize using Designated Initializers ---\n  SecureCommandShaHeader req_hdr{\n      .algo \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(wrapped_algo_),\n      .payload_len \u003d ToLittleEndian(static_cast\u003cuint32_t\u003e(data.size()))\n  };\n  SHA256(data.data(), data.size(), req_hdr.hash.data());\n\n  // Allocate exactly once\n  std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e cmd(sizeof(req_hdr) + data.size());\n  \n  if (!WriteStruct(std::span{cmd}, req_hdr)) {\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kBufferTooSmall);\n  }\n  // Copy payload directly after the header\n  std::memcpy(cmd.data() + sizeof(req_hdr), data.data(), data.size());\n\n  // --- 2. Send Data ---\n  auto ret_res \u003d DoUsbTransfer(cmd, sizeof(SecureShaResponseHeader), timeout);\n  if (!ret_res) return std::unexpected(ret_res.error());\n  auto \u0026ret \u003d ret_res.value();\n\n  // --- 3. Deserialize safely ---\n  auto rsp_hdr_res \u003d ReadStruct\u003cSecureShaResponseHeader\u003e(ret);\n  if (!rsp_hdr_res) {\n    return std::unexpected(BootromError::kResponseTruncated);\n  }\n  SecureShaResponseHeader rsp_hdr \u003d rsp_hdr_res.value();\n\n  uint32_t rsp_wrapped_len \u003d FromLittleEndian(rsp_hdr.payload_len);\n  if (rsp_wrapped_len \u003e ret.size() - sizeof(SecureShaResponseHeader)) {\n    rsp_wrapped_len \u003d ret.size() - sizeof(SecureShaResponseHeader);\n  }\n\n  auto rsp_payload \u003d std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e(ret).subspan(\n      sizeof(SecureShaResponseHeader), rsp_wrapped_len);\n\n  return std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e(rsp_payload.begin(), rsp_payload.end());\n}\n\n```\n\n#### Implementation C: Basic Flash Commands (`bootrom.cc`)\n\nSend simple commands directly from the stack without allocating vectors at all.\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, BootromError\u003e BootromComm::EraseFlash(uint32_t offset,\n                                                          uint32_t size) {\n  std::println(\"--- Executing CMD_FLASH_ERASE (0x22) ---\");\n\n  // Build the packet on the stack\n  EgisBootrom::FlashEraseCommand req{\n      .address \u003d ToLittleEndian(EgisBootrom::kFlashBaseAddr + offset),\n      .size \u003d ToLittleEndian(size)\n  };\n\n  // Treat the stack struct as a byte span\n  // (Casting FROM a struct pointer TO const uint8_t* is legal and safe)\n  std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e req_span(reinterpret_cast\u003cconst uint8_t*\u003e(\u0026req), sizeof(req));\n\n  auto resp \u003d SendBootromCommand(req_span, kFlashTimeout);\n  if (!resp) {\n    std::println(stderr, \"Erase Failed!\");\n    return std::unexpected(resp.error());\n  }\n  \n  std::println(\"Erase Success: {} bytes at 0x{:08x}\", size, req.address);\n  return {};\n}\n\n```",
